**The work is not blocked: I found nothing that has already been done in a way this stage should reuse, already failed, or contradicts the plan. Verdict `novel`.** The three observations below do not block it.

**PART A — Direction**
- **A1 (already decided), not a blocking finding.** The direction was decided upstream, and the plan follows those decisions rather than repeating them:
  - `docs/d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md:2040-2044` (PD228(g)(h)): the retire list is the 6 register pairs, L2-R1 is its own saved file, and the gates are listed there.
  - `:1931` (PD226(d)): `#9018/#9025` and `#29505/#29512` were put on L2-R's retire list.
  - `:487` (PD189(b)): the old `#119/#2972` is retired in L2-R "after the live-consumer check".
  - `:1891`: `#7311` stays on w11389 until L2-R.
  - The plan's gates match `:2044`. The Error List condition "no new class, loses only retired items" is covered by the post-stage expected-file comparison.
- **A2 (already refuted): nothing found.** No archived review or retrospective argues against retiring these pairs.
- **A3 (contradicted): nothing blocking.** One point to watch:
  - `tools/bench/opmodels/delete_object.json:217-218` marks **only_sink as AMBIGUOUS**. When the deleted node was a wire's only sink, w8385 was deleted outright, but w2160/w2187 were kept as half-wires.
  - The plan's gate D requires the real wires lost to equal exactly the 11 stubs the simulation drops.
  - Per the plan's own face table, those 11 stubs lose every terminal (Right inner = sink on a sourceless stub; Left inner = source on a stub with no sink). The ambiguous cases in the model had a surviving source, so the model does not directly contradict this. If gate D mismatches, look here first.
- **A4 (unread evidence): nothing missing.**
  - The plan cites `stage_d1_l7_r.json` (#15/#51) and the opmodel.
  - The recipe header (`tools/recipes/stage_d1_l2r1.py:4-5`) cites `stage_d1_l7_r.py` and the new code in `tools/stagexec.py:2460-2463` says what existed before it.

**PART B — The file this stage builds**
- **B1 (already built) / B3 (existing helper), Q1:** a register-pair retirement with a live-consumer gate and a Remove Bad Wires gate has been built and measured before, in `tools/recipes/stage_d1_l7_r.py:94-101`:
  - It checks live consumers with `V.reach4` **once, before all deletes**.
  - It then deletes with `s.delete_object`, and its gate "RBW removed no live uid edge" passed: `tools/bench/stage_d1_l7_r.log:420-425,448` (bad 7, ExecState 1).
  - `stage_d1_m3a4.py:13,81-87` also retired 8 objects and then ran Remove Bad Wires.

  The new `RetireGuardBE` differs in three ways, which is why I do not raise this as blocking:
  - It re-reads the graph **before each delete**, as PD228(g) requires ("re-read … BEFORE each delete"). L7-R's gate cannot do that.
  - It runs inside the Executor.
  - It works on terminal rows instead of a built G4 graph.

  `retire_check` only looks at direct sinks outside the pair, while `reach4` follows the whole downstream graph. For a pair deleted as a unit, direct sinks are enough. Reusing `reach4` would mean building a G4 graph on every read, and it is not required. `retire_ends` has no earlier equivalent. The closest is L7-R's edge-set equality at `:100-101`, which is coarser.
- **B2 (already failed): nothing found.** The same retirement pattern succeeded twice in L7-R (r1 and r2) and in M3a-4.
- **B4 (already measured), Q2 and Q3:**
  - **Q2:** nothing on record says that deleting a Right register leaves a "Wire has loose ends" item on a branched net.
    - `delete_object.json:215` says: "a branched wire it sank keeps its other sinks".
    - L7-R's Remove Bad Wires removed no live edge after deleting SR pairs (`stage_d1_l7_r.log:448`).
    - The only warning about Remove Bad Wires trimming loose ends on kept wires is `tools/bench/cards/result_98-2.json:16`. It concerns `move_into_frame`, not deletes, and the plan's check that Remove Bad Wires hits "no shared live net" covers it anyway.
  - **Q3:** no later stage needs any of the 12 uids.
    - `split-plan:2036`: all 12 have 0 live consumers.
    - `:2039`: the 16 open rows are all QRT-owned, and none of them is an SR uid.
    - `:1931`: the new 1.2 pairs were re-made by B2-09..14 from `#9227`/`#29616`, not from `#9018`/`#29505`.
    - `:2037`: L2-R2's 13 tunnels do not include any of the 12.

